Public buyers publish the result of every tender above certain thresholds. Each notice names the winning supplier, the value and, often, the duration of the contract. That is not an opinion or a survey — it is a record of money that will be spent.

{% split label="01 — The data" title="Where the money" titleAccent="is committed" image="/src/assets/img/platform/cascade-room.jpg" imageAlt="A meeting room with people around a table" %}
In 2026, transport, energy and digital services took the largest share of the awards recorded so far. These figures are placeholders for the sample article, not real data.

Because many contracts run for several years, the awards signed today describe activity that will continue well into 2027 and 2028.
{% /split %}

## Why awards are an early signal

A contract award comes before the invoices, the hiring and the purchases from subcontractors. When a region signs large infrastructure contracts, suppliers, materials and services will be needed there next.

{% split label="02 — The method" title="From notices to" titleAccent="market signals" image="/src/assets/img/platform/vision-workshop.jpg" imageAlt="A small workshop seen through a shop window" reverse=true %}
datixxia™ groups awards by sector, location and period, so that you can compare where commitments are growing and where they are slowing down.

The result is not a promise about the future, but evidence about what has already been decided.
{% /split %}

{% takeaway text="Awarded public contracts show where money is already committed — and therefore where activity is likely to develop next." /%}

## What this means for your organisation

Use awards as one input among others: they complement financial statements, sector studies and your own knowledge of the market.
